lesson k-cooking-changes v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=8 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Cooking changes things"
  goal "Say what heat does to food when it is cooked, tell raw from cooked, and say which changes can go back."

  look {
    scene 18x12 {
      beforeafter ch change="toast-bread" show=1 names=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      Heat changes food. Soft white bread becomes brown, crisp toast. A runny egg becomes firm. A
      runny cake mixture becomes a cake. These changes cannot go back: toast never turns into bread
      again. Melted butter is different, because it sets again when it cools.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.raw-or-cooked k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice k.raw-or-cooked count=2 seed=2
    practice k.what-heat-did count=2 seed=3
    practice k.toast-back count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    practice k.hot-oven count=2 seed=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.melt-or-cook k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.bake-in-order
  }
  remember {
    say "Cooking changes food, and it cannot go back. Hot things are for grown-ups."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Cooking together is the lesson, with the grown-up at the hob and the
    oven; the child watches, stirs cold mixtures and tastes. The idea to watch is that every change
    can be undone; set a melted chocolate button beside a piece of toast. If the child is stuck, look
    at a raw and a cooked egg together. Off the page, make toast and ask what the toaster did.
    England, Reception, ELG The Natural World (recalled, not fetched): "Understand some important
    processes and changes in the natural world around them, including the seasons and changing
    states of matter".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level dry pasta is worked in the teacher's pen first, the changes are toast and an egg, and the change that goes back is melted butter."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the changes are a cake and rising dough, freezing sits beside baking, and the change that goes back is melted chocolate."
  }
}
